      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340712",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}."
    },
