      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-340719",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{-\\frac{7}{3}}=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}."
